ree tiles of the same suit in sequence. I have exactly one W2, one W3, one W4. That is a valid Chow set if they were grouped. So if I keep them, I have a potential concealed Chow. So discarding any of them would break that.\n\nBut currently, they are not a meld; they are just three tiles. To declare a Chow, I need to complete it by drawing a tile or claiming a discard. But since they are already one each, I don't need any additional tile to have the set; I already have the set. However, in hand, they are just three separate tiles; they can be used as a Chow when forming the final hand. So they are a potential group.\n\nTherefore, I should keep W2, W3, W4 together as they form a natural Chow candidate.\n\nThus, tiles to consider discarding are the ones not part of potential groups: T4, T8, T9, B2, B4. Among these, T9 is dead, so discard T9 is optimal.\n\nAlso, safety: T9 has been discarded multiple times, so it's safe.\n\nSo my choice is to discard T9.\n\nReason: T9 is a terminal with no remaining copies, making it useless for improving my hand. Keeping it offers no value for forming pairs or sequences. Meanwhile, preserving the W2-W3-W4 sequence maintains a potential Chow, and other tiles like T4, T8, B2, B4 have more live copies and possible connections. Discarding T9 is also safe given its discards by multiple players.\n\nAnswer: Play T9"}
