secutive; I could form Chow sequences. For example, W2-W3-W4 Chow if I draw W1 or W4? Actually I have one each of W2, W3, W4, W5. With exposed Chow [W4,W5,W6], I have W4 and W5 used there. So my hand W4 and W5 are separate? Wait, the meld [W4, W5, W6] is exposed, meaning those tiles are no longer in my hand. So my hand W4 and W5 are additional copies? Let's recount: My hand tiles listed: W2:1, W3:1, W4:1, W5:1. So yes, I have one each of W2, W3, W4, W5. With the exposed Chow using W4 and W5, I have extra W4 and W5 in hand. So I have W2, W3, W4, W5 in hand. These could form Chow combinations: e.g., W2-W3-W4 Chow if I get an extra W4? Actually I need three tiles: I have W2, W3, W4 (that's a Chow), but I only have one W4, so I can't use the same tile twice. So I need another W4 to complete Chow? No, Chow requires three distinct tiles: for W2-W3-W4, I need one W2, one W3, one W4. I have one each, so that is a complete Chow set in hand already. Similarly, W3-W4-W5 is also possible. So I have flexible W chows. To improve, I might want to keep these W tiles to form melds. Discarding a W might break potential. Meanwhile, B tiles are not contributing to Half Flush if I aim for W+H. So discarding a B tile is better. Among B2 and B4, B2 I have a pair, which could be a potential pair for the hand. Keeping a pair is valuable. B4 is a single, so discarding B4 is safer and maintains the B2 pair. Also, B4 is a simple tile, not dangerous. J2 is an honor, discarding it might be slightly more risky if opponents need dragons, but there are only 2 left. Considering all, discarding B4 seems optimal: it reduces suits, keeps W flexibility, keeps a pair (B2), and keeps J2 for possible Dragon Pung or Half Flush inclusion.\n\nAnswer: Play B4"}
