ly, pung if I get a third of something.\n\nSo potentially, I could aim for: Meld1: exposed [W4,W5,W6], Meld2: exposed [T1,T2,T3], Meld3: from hand [W2,W3,W4] (if I use hand W4), Meld4: from hand [W4,W5,W6] (using the extras), Pair: B2. But that uses W4 three times? Actually, I have only one extra W4 in hand. Let's inventory hand tiles exactly: \nW2 (1), W3 (1), W4 (1), W5 (1), W6 (1), T3 (1), B2 (2). So only one each of W4,W5,W6. So I cannot form a second W4,W5,W6 chow because I need three tiles. So that's not possible.\n\nBetter plan: Use hand to form one chow: W2,W3,W4. That uses W2,W3,W4. Then I have left: W5, W6, T3, B2, B2. That's 5 tiles. I need one more meld and a pair. The pair could be B2. Then I need a meld from W5,W6,T3. That could be a chow if I get W7 to form W5,W6,W7, or a pung if I get another W5 or W6 or T3. So I am waiting for W7 or another W5/W6/T3.\n\nGiven W7 is exhausted (0 left), waiting for W7 is impossible. So that plan is dead. \n\nAlternative: Form a chow W4,W5,W6 from hand? But I already have that as an exposed meld; I don't have three of them in hand. So not possible.\n\nMaybe form a pung: I have only singles, so no pung yet.\n\nSo currently, my hand is not close to a ready hand. I need to improve shape. That means discarding a tile that is less useful and drawing better tiles.\n\nGiven the Half Flush idea, I want to keep W tiles and discard non-W. Non-W are T3 and B2. B2 is a pair, so discarding one B2 breaks the pair. But having a pair of B2 is not helpful for a W-based hand because I would need a pair in W eventually. So breaking the B2 pair might be okay if I can form a pair from W tiles. I have many W singles: W2,W3,W4,W5,W6. I could pair any of them if I draw another. So discarding one B2 is reasonable.\n\nDiscarding T3 also removes a non-W tile. Which is better? T3 has 3 left unseen (from tile distribution). B2 has 3 left unseen. Both are equally available. But B2 is a pair, so keeping both B2 gives me a ready pair, which is valuable for any hand. If I discard one B2, I lose that and have to form a new pair. With many W singles, forming a pair might be easier. But having a ready pair saves a step. However, for Half Flush, the pair must be W or honor. So B2 pair doesn't count. So ultimately, I will need a W pair. Therefore, keeping the B2 pair is not necessary for Half Flush. So I can discard a B2.\n\nBut also consider the potential for other patterns: All Types (6 points) requires all suits and honors. I have W, T, B already, and could get honors. That might be possible if I keep a mix. But my hand currently has no honors. Discarding B2 reduces B suit presence. I have only B2 left, so keeping one B2 maintains B suit for All Types. But I need a honor pung or pair for All Types? Actually, All Types requires tiles from all three suits, wind tiles, and dragon tiles. So I need at least one wind and one dragon. I have none now. That's a long shot.\n\nHalf Flush seems more direct. So I'll commit to Half Flush.\n\nThus, discard a non-W tile. Between T3 and B2, which is safer? Looking at discard history: T3 has been played by previous player (Play T3), and next player hasn't played many T3. B2 has been played by previous player (Play B2). Both seem safe. But B2 is a pair, discarding one might risk giving someone a pung? B2 has 3 left unseen, so there are two more B2 out there; someone could have a pair waiting. But that's always a risk. T3 also similar.\n\nGiven I have two B2, discarding one leaves me with one B2, which is a single. If I discard T3, I keep both B2, maintaining a pair that could be used if I abandon Half Flush. But I think Half Flush is the best path, so I'll discard B2 to reduce suit clutter.\n\nBut let's check tile distribution: W tiles available: W1(1), W2(3), W3(3), W4(1), W5(3), W6(3), W7(0), W8(0), W9(1). So W2,W3,W5,W6 have good availability. I can potentially get pairs or chows. W4 is scarce (1 left), but I already have one W4 in hand and one in meld? Actually my hand has a W4, and meld has a W4, so that's two W4s used. There's one W4 left unseen. So I could get a third W4 for a pung? Possibly.\n\nGiven all, I think discarding B2 is a good step toward Half Flush.\n\nHowever, one more consideration: my valid actions include Play W4, Play W5, etc. That means I could discard a W tile instead. Discarding a W tile might be bad for Half Flush, but if that W tile is isolated, maybe. For example, W2 is part of a potential chow with W3,W4. W3 similarly. W4 is useful. W5 and W6 are also useful. All W tiles are useful. So discarding a W tile would be wasteful.\n\nTherefore, the best discard is either T3 or B2. I choose B2 to keep the T3, which might combine with my exposed T1,T2,T3 meld? Actually, I have an exposed meld of T1,T2,T3, so T3 is already used. My hand T3 is an extra. It could be used to form a pair if I get another T3, but that would not contribute to Half Flush. So T3 is also not helpful for Half Flush. So both are equally unhelpful. But discarding B2 reduces the B suit count, which is good for Half Flush since Half Flush allows honors, but B is a suit, so no B tiles allowed. So discard B2.\n\nI'll discard one B2.\n\nThus, my final choice: Play B2.\n\nAnswer format: Play B2\n\nSituation: I have a 14-tile hand including two exposed chows. My hand tiles include multiple W tiles and non-W tiles. Aiming for a Half Flush (6 points) is feasible given W tile availability.\nEvaluation: Discarding a non-W tile advances the Half Flush plan. Between T3 and B2, B2 is a pair but not needed for Half Flush.\nReason: Committing to Half Flush by reducing other suits increases chances to form W-based melds and pair. Discarding B2 is a safe move given tile distribution and maintains flexibility with T3.\nAnswer: Play B2"}
