sequences. W2,W3,W4 is a potential chow, so keep those. T5 pair is good. T3 might combine with T4,T5 if I draw T4 (1 left), but unlikely.  \nSo between T1 and W6, which is safer? Looking at discard history: Previous player discarded W5, W9, etc. Next player discarded W4, W7. Across player discarded W3, W9. Many W tiles have been discarded. W6 hasn't been discarded much (history shows no W6 discards? Actually, in my history I played W9, W8, W5; next player played W4, W1, W5; across played W1, W9, W3; previous played W4, W9, W8, W7, W5. No W6 discards. So W6 might be relatively safe. T1 has been discarded by previous player (Play T1) and across player (Play T1). So T1 might also be safe.  \nI think T1 is the best discard to move towards a more balanced hand.  \n\nReason: Discarding T1 removes a terminal tile, which helps potential patterns like All Simples. It keeps the T5 pair intact and preserves the W2-W3-W4 sequence for a possible chow. It also avoids discarding a tile that could be useful for sequences (T1 is only useful with T2,T3, which are scarce). Given the tile distribution and my current hand structure, T1 is the least useful tile.  \n\nAnswer: Play T1"}
